    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large language models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (e.g., attention projections, MLP layers) across devices. Every device holds a *shard* of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a *complete* copy of a subset of experts, and *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nTP is typically used for dense layers (attention, embeddings, dense MLPs) and EP for the MoE blocks. Production inference stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-MI, Megatron-based servers) combine them: e.g., TP=4 for attention/dense parts, EP=8 for the FFN MoE layers, often with data parallelism (DP) replicated across the whole model for throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The standard Megatron-style layout for TP degree `T`:\n\n**Attention block:**\n- `W_Q, W_K, V` of shape `(d_model, 3\u00b7d_head\u00b7n_heads)` are split along the head dimension: each rank owns `n_heads / T` heads. QKV projection is *column-parallel* (output dimension split).\n- Output projection `W_O` of shape `(d_model, d_model)` is *row-parallel* (input dimension split): each rank multiplies its attention output slice by a `(d_model/T, d_model)` shard.\n\n**MLP block:**\n- Up/gate projections",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
